module bindings.go.optionalTokenClaimValidation;

// RFC 7519's registered `iss` and RFC 7662's introspection response both make the
// issuer a claim the *token side* supplies, and both leave validating it to the
// relying party: OpenID Connect Core 3.1.3.7 requires the issuer to match the
// expected issuer exactly. A relying party that compares the two only when the
// token actually carries an issuer has made the check optional for whoever mints
// the token, and a token with no issuer claim -- from another authorization
// server, or from an introspection endpoint that omits the field -- then passes
// issuer validation on nothing at all (CWE-287/CWE-290).
//
// Three facts together are that shape. A guard on the *claim* being non-empty
// (`iss != ""`, `claims.Issuer != ""`, `tokenIssuer != ""`); a `!=` comparison
// whose two sides are both issuer-named, which is the mismatch rejection the
// guard is wrapped around (`iss != a.issuer`, `claims.Issuer != v.expectedIssuer`,
// `iss != v.trustedIssuer`); and nothing in the same body establishing that the
// claim is empty. That last one is the neutraliser, and it is the documented
// fail-closed form however it is spelled -- an empty or zero-length issuer claim
// recognised anywhere in the body is what a handler needs in order to refuse one.
//
// Two nearby shapes are deliberately not this, and both were measured on real
// code before the mismatch-comparison fact was required.
//
// Reading a configured issuer out of settings and acting on it only when it is
// set -- `if cfg.Issuer != ""` -- is ordinary provider construction and says
// nothing about any token, so the presence guard must name the claim rather than
// the configuration. golang-jwt's Validator.Validate is the harder version of the
// same thing: `if v.expectedIss != ""` calls verifyIssuer with required=true, and
// verifyIssuer refuses an empty `iss` in a sibling function. The presence guard
// there is on the expectation, not on the claim, and no comparison of the two
// happens in that body at all -- which is what the mismatch-comparison fact
// distinguishes.
//
// Both capitalisations of each side of the comparison are listed because the
// engine matches binary-expression operands case-sensitively, and Go spells the
// claim either `iss` or `Iss` and the expectation either `issuer` or `Issuer`.
binding goOptionalTokenClaimValidation {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function" and node.context.language == "go" and containsAny(node.context.binary, ["iss!=\"\"", "tokenIssuer!=\"\"", "claimIssuer!=\"\"", "claims.Issuer!=\"\"", "token.Issuer!=\"\"", "payload.Issuer!=\"\""]) and containsAny(node.context.binary, ["iss!=issuer", "iss!=Issuer", "Iss!=issuer", "Iss!=Issuer"]) and not containsAny(node.context.binary, ["iss==\"\"", "tokenIssuer==\"\"", "claimIssuer==\"\"", "claims.Issuer==\"\"", "token.Issuer==\"\"", "payload.Issuer==\"\"", "len(iss)==0", "len(iss)<1"])
  emit issue code.OptionalTokenClaimValidation at node
}
